{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"182e5d0f\" lines=\"89-103\">\n<description>\nMove each gray marker along its green path until it reaches the cell next to a black-green-black gate, erasing the path behind it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground components are made from a path color, a paired gate-end color, and single marker pixels.\n- A gate is a straight end-center-end motif whose center has the path color and whose two ends share the gate-end color.\n\nRule:\n- In every 4-connected foreground component with exactly one marker and one gate, erase the marker and all path-color pixels except the gate center.\n- Place the marker on the unique path-color pixel adjacent to the gate center but not between the two gate ends.\n- Components without both a marker and a gate stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The black-green-black motif marks the stopping gate; the gray marker consumes the connected green path until it rests on the path cell immediately beside that gate.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"cf5fd0ad\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nBuild a four-quadrant rotational tiling of the input square.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square grid treated as one complete sprite.\n\nRule\n- Each output quadrant is a 2x2 repetition of a rotated copy of the whole input.\n- The quadrant rotations are upper-left 180 degrees, upper-right 90 degrees\n  clockwise, lower-left 90 degrees counterclockwise, and lower-right unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is a 4x4 grid of original-size blocks, not a per-pixel scale-up.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"abc82100\" lines=\"70-86\">\n<description>\nUse local source-target-pattern examples to replace matching source pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with isolated colored pixels and teal (8) pattern components.\n- A rule example is a 4-neighbor colored pair source->target whose continued\n  direction reaches an anchor touching a teal pattern component.\n\nRule:\n- For each rule example, copy that teal component onto every other pixel of\n  source.color, recoloring the copied component to target.color.\n- Translation maps the rule anchor onto the source pixel being replaced.\n- Remove teal components and all pixels used by rule examples.\n\nKey insight: The two adjacent colored pixels define the color substitution and\nthe anchor point; the nearby teal component is the stencil that gets recolored.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c7dc4dd\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nLearn a transformation rule from a training pair of framed sprites, then apply it to a test sprite.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid contains several colored square frames (hollow borders of a single color).\n- Each frame's interior holds a small pattern sprite; one frame's interior is blank (all black).\n- Two frames form a training pair (their interiors share the same color palette); one remaining frame is the test case.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the training pair by matching interior color sets; the other non-blank frame is the test input.\n- Infer the rule mapping source→destination (e.g. reflection, color inversion, gap filling, endpoint extraction, or checkerboard complement).\n- Apply that same rule to the test sprite; output the transformed interior.\n\nKey insight: The puzzle is an analogy — \"A is to B as C is to ?\" — where you must discover the visual rule from one example pair and generalize it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5792cb4d\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nReverse the sequence of colors along the single 4-connected foreground path while keeping the path shape fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All non-background pixels form one multicolor 4-connected one-pixel-wide path.\n- The path has exactly two endpoints and no branches.\n\nRule:\n- Walk the path from one endpoint to the other.\n- Replace the colors on those same path cells by the original path colors in reverse order.\n- Leave every background pixel unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Nothing moves geometrically; only the color string written on the path is reversed between the endpoints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8abad3cf\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nRepack each non-background color group as a solid square ordered by increasing size.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one dominant background color, and each other color has a\nperfect-square pixel count. Those square side lengths are unique.\n\nRule\nConvert every non-background color into a filled square whose side length is\nthe square root of its pixel count. Place the squares from left to right in\nincreasing side length, bottom-aligned, with one background column between\nneighboring squares.\n\nKey insight\nThe original geometry of each color is discarded; only the color's total\npixel count determines the size of its packed output square.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8cc533f\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nComplete every colored sprite by applying the union of all aligned partial sprite masks.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Each non-background color appears as a separated partial copy of one common sprite mask.\n- The copies share a top-left-aligned sprite window; some copies may be missing lower or right edge pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop each same-color mask to its bounding box.\n- Pad the crops to the maximum observed height and width, aligned at the top-left, and OR them into one complete template.\n- Fill that template at every sprite location using the sprite's own color.\n\nKey insight:\n- No single input sprite is complete, but their missing cells are complementary, so the aligned union reveals the full mask.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b25e450b\" lines=\"28-43\">\n<description>\nSlide each black border rectangle to the opposite border, recoloring its swept stripe orange and leaving the rectangle black at the far edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains black rectangular 4-connected masks, each touching exactly one outer border.\n- Grey cells form incidental pattern content; orange is the trail color used when a black mask slides.\n\nRule\n- Treat all black rectangles as moving simultaneously.\n- A rectangle touching the top or bottom edge moves vertically to the opposite edge in the same columns.\n- A rectangle touching the left or right edge moves horizontally to the opposite edge in the same rows.\n- Recolor the complete swept row or column stripe to orange, then stamp every destination rectangle in black.\n\nKey insight\n- Edge contact gives the slide direction; the output is the set of opposite-edge black rectangles plus their orange trails.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"446ef5d2\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered rectangular pieces into a rectangle at an L-marker corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Non-bg rectangular pieces scattered on the grid.\n- 3-pixel L-marker (2x2 minus one cell) tags the corner; the gap is the corner cell.\n\nRule:\n- rect_col = most common color across all piece pixels; all outer rectangle edges must be rect_col.\n- Translate pieces (no rotation) to tile the rectangle; touching edges must match, with rect_col as wildcard.\n\nKey insight: rect_col acts as wildcard during edge matching — only non-rect_col pixels at shared boundaries must agree.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88e364bc\" lines=\"102-115\">\n<description>\nSlide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n\nRule:\n- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n\nKey insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"50c07299\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nMove the diagonal foreground segment to the next longer run along the same up-right diagonal.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and one foreground color. The foreground pixels form a\ncontiguous one-pixel-wide segment on a single anti-diagonal.\n\nRule\nErase the current segment. If its columns are c0..c1 and its length is L, draw the same\ncolor on columns c1+1..c1+L+1 along the same anti-diagonal.\n\nKey insight\nThe segment advances past its current upper-right end by one whole segment position, and\nthe next segment is one pixel longer than the current one.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1478ab18\" lines=\"35-43\">\n<description>\nPaint the teal outline of the right triangle determined by the missing corner of the grey-marker square.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is an orange background with exactly four foreground marker pixels of one color. The markers have a square bounding box: three markers are bbox corners, and the fourth marker is inside that square.\nRule\nFind the one corner of the marker bounding square that has no marker. That missing corner is the right-angle vertex of a right isosceles triangle; paint its two axis-aligned legs and the opposite diagonal in teal, leaving existing marker pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\nThe present corner marker opposite the missing corner is a decoy; the three triangle vertices are the missing corner and the two marker corners adjacent to it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4888269\" lines=\"67-88\">\n<description>\nRecolor every target pixel by following the ordered two-column substitution legend.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background surrounds one multicolor vertical legend: a connected,\n  fully filled two-column block with one color pair per row.\n- Other non-background pixels or objects are the targets; divider or frame\n  pixels are ordinary target-side structure that remain unchanged unless their\n  color is rewritten by the legend.\n\nRule\n- Read the legend rows from top to bottom as substitutions from the left\n  column color to the right column color.\n- For each non-background pixel outside the legend, scan those rows in order;\n  whenever the current color equals the row's left color, replace it with the\n  row's right color and keep scanning lower rows.\n- Leave the legend itself and background pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The same color may be rewritten multiple times, but only by substitutions\n  that appear later in the vertical legend, so the row order is part of the\n  symbol instruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7ec998c9\" lines=\"14-28\">\n<description>\nDraw a blue folded corner-to-corner route through the lone marker's column.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid filled with one background color.\n- Exactly one non-background marker pixel; blue is absent from the input.\n\nRule:\n- Preserve the marker and repaint background pixels in its column blue from top to bottom.\n- If the marker is in the unique center column, connect that column to the top-right and bottom-left corners.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
